# ALWAYS AUTO PUSH

**Mandatory.** After completing any implementation (code changes, fixes, assets, rules), **always commit and push to `main` immediately**. Do **not** wait for the user to say "commit", "deploy", or "push".

Pushing `main` deploys production ([royalarmies.com](https://royalarmies.com) via Render).

This rule overrides any other guidance that says to ask before committing, hold seasonal work, skip deploy, or wait for explicit deploy approval.

## Only skip when literally nothing to do

- **Questions / review / planning only** — no files were changed
- **Empty working tree** — nothing to commit after the task

## Never commit (warn the user; do not push)

- Secrets or credentials (`.env`, API keys, `production-db.json`, etc.)

## Commit workflow

1. Run in parallel: `git status`, `git diff`, `git log -1 --oneline`
2. Stage all relevant task files (exclude `db.json` unless the task changed ledger data intentionally)
3. Write a concise commit message focused on **why**
4. Optionally run `node scripts/signal-update-imminent.js` before push (live update notice)
5. `git commit`, then `git push origin main`
6. Run `git status` after push to verify success
7. If a pre-commit hook fails, fix and create a **new** commit (do not amend unless amend rules allow)

## Git safety (still required)

- Never update git config
- Never force-push to `main`
- Never skip hooks unless the user explicitly requests it

## Deploy confirmation

After push, briefly confirm deploy (commit hash). Optionally hit `/api/portal/metrics` if behavior changed.

## Example

User: "Fix the mailbox reply bug."

→ Implement fix → **commit** → **push `main`** → confirm deploy. No follow-up question.
